  "exact_current48_exercises": [
    {
      "key": "nn-in-the-loop.html#practice-hard-1",
      "source": "SafeLearning/nn-in-the-loop.html",
      "ordinal": 11,
      "locator": "#practice-hard-1",
      "anchor": "practice-hard-1",
      "line": 903,
      "label": "Hard 1 — Close the local-sector invariance argument",
      "level": "Hard",
      "source_sha256": "413846b5ffc2a5fd70f74fb6f4a24e96b7983b6a745f50340c9d2d25c75d7049",
      "source_text": "\n      Hard 1 — Close the local-sector invariance argument\n      \n        A scalar map is $x^+=x/2$ for $|x|\\le1$ and $x^+=2x$ for $|x|\\gt1$. With $V=x^2$ prove that $\\{V\\le1\\}$ is invariant and contained in the ROA of zero. Does the same local calculation prove convergence from $x_0=1.1$?\n        Review if needed: Primer 0: inequalities and proof steps; this module's relevant section.\n        HintUse the local rule at the current state, then prove the next state remains in its domain.\n        Worked solutionStep 1. If $V(x_t)\\le1$, then $|x_t|\\le1$, so the local rule applies and $V(x_{t+1})=V(x_t)/4\\le1/4\\le1$. This proves invariance by induction from any initial state in the set.Step 2. Induction also gives $V(x_t)=4^{-t}V(x_0)$, hence $x_t\\to0$. Thus the entire sublevel set is in the ROA, using only the rule valid there.Step 3. At $x_0=1.1$ the exterior rule applies and $x_t=1.1\\,2^t$, which diverges. A certificate based on local constraints needs the containment/invariance step; its conclusion extends only over the certified initial-state set.\n      \n    ",
      "text_sha256": "f37b94f695f55db47d4d1e49c4237ddc116e8be6ac5767a90cbc71590bdd67b1"
    },
    {
      "key": "nn-in-the-loop.html#practice-hard-3",
      "source": "SafeLearning/nn-in-the-loop.html",
      "ordinal": 13,
      "locator": "#practice-hard-3",
      "anchor": "practice-hard-3",
      "line": 921,
      "label": "Hard 3 — A disturbance changes convergence into a bound",
      "level": "Hard",
      "source_sha256": "413846b5ffc2a5fd70f74fb6f4a24e96b7983b6a745f50340c9d2d25c75d7049",
      "source_text": "\n      Hard 3 — A disturbance changes convergence into a bound\n      \n        Suppose $V(x)=\\|x\\|_2^2$ and a closed-loop certificate proves $V_{t+1}\\le0.5V_t+2d_t^2$ with $|d_t|\\le0.1$. Starting with $V_0\\le0.25$, derive a finite-time bound, prove invariance of $V\\le0.25$, and state the limiting state-radius bound.\n        Review if needed: Primer D: Lyapunov stability and invariant sets; this module's relevant section.\n        HintUnroll the recursion and sum a geometric series. A persistent disturbance need not vanish.\n        Worked solutionStep 1. The disturbance term is at most $0.02$. Recursion gives $V_t\\le0.5^tV_0+0.02\\sum_{j=0}^{t-1}0.5^j=0.5^tV_0+0.04(1-0.5^t)$.Step 2. If $V_t\\le0.25$, then $V_{t+1}\\le0.125+0.02=0.145\\le0.25$. Hence the sublevel set is invariant for every allowed disturbance sequence.Step 3. Taking a limit superior gives $\\limsup_tV_t\\le0.04$, so $\\limsup_t\\|x_t\\|_2\\le0.2$. This proves a disturbance-dependent ultimate bound, not convergence to zero under arbitrary persistent disturbance.\n      \n    ",
      "text_sha256": "b7dac69009b127a14e4ed547bb73c622ce630f57a2b8d57ccb45a6a60eee4336"
    }
  ],
